# Let members of the dolphone group flash the Flipper One's LEDs while a call
# rings (crates/phoned/src/leds.rs). LED files in sysfs are root's, so give the
# group the trigger and brightness on "add", and on "change": the kernel sends a
# change uevent each time an LED's trigger is set (__led_trigger_set in
# drivers/leds/led-triggers.c), after creating the trigger's own files, which is
# when the timer trigger's delay_on and delay_off appear, and the netdev trigger's
# files when phoned gives an LED back to it: Flipper's image points the network LEDs
# at their interfaces (99-network-leds.rules), and the kernel forgets that when
# the trigger changes, so phoned writes device_name and the modes back. The same
# chgrp in a udev RUN is how brightnessctl's rules open LEDs to a group.
#
# udev expands $ itself, so the shell's $f is written $$f (udevadm verify checks it).
#
# Another machine's LEDs: copy this file to /etc/udev/rules.d/, change KERNEL to
# match the names in the device profile's ring_leds, and reload the rules.
ACTION=="add|change", SUBSYSTEM=="leds", KERNEL=="flipper-one:*", RUN+="/bin/sh -c 'cd /sys%p && for f in trigger brightness delay_on delay_off device_name interval link link_10 link_100 link_1000 link_2500 link_5000 link_10000 half_duplex full_duplex rx tx rx_err tx_err; do [ -e $$f ] && chgrp dolphone $$f && chmod g+w $$f; done; true'"
